{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"═══ ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND PROVEN BY EXPERIMENT — and it is NOT in any of the three suspected seams ═══\n\n**`PydanticAIRunner.run` DISCARDS `req.config` and resolves the model from the config it was\nCONSTRUCTED with.** `runner.py:239` is `cfg = self._config`, and `runner.py:283` is\n`resolved = _pai_model(cfg)`. So the per-step model that `RunnerAgentStep` carefully computes into\n`req.config.model` is never consulted; every call in a gate run uses the single config baked at\ngate-dispatch time (`gate_dispatch.py:411` `runner_sel = request.runner or get_runner(cfg)`).\n\nTHE EXPERIMENT (direct, decisive, no LLM spend): construct `PydanticAIRunner(cfg(model=\n\"CONSTRUCTOR-MODEL\"))`, call `run(RunRequest(config=cfg(model=\"PER-STEP-MODEL\")))` inside a gate\nsession, and spy on `_pai_model`. Result: `req.config.model == \"PER-STEP-MODEL\"` while the model\nhanded to `_pai_model` is `\"CONSTRUCTOR-MODEL\"`. The declaration is computed correctly and then\nthrown away one layer below where anyone had looked.\n\n**THIS EXPLAINS EVERY OBSERVATION, including the one that looked contradictory.** Nine live calls\nall on cfg.model; `verify`'s `model: standard` among them; pre-existing rather than a 3768\nregression; and both halves of the plumbing \"correct in isolation\" — because they ARE correct. The\ndefect is downstream of both.\n\n**A PREVIOUSLY-ELIMINATED HYPOTHESIS WAS ELIMINATED ON FAULTY REASONING — recording the correction\nbecause it is what kept the search away from this seam for three sessions.** H3 (class-table\nfallback) was dismissed with \"an empty table gives `standard` → SONNET, but the log showed OPUS\".\nTwo things were missed:\n 1. `_parse_slot` (`model_classes.py:158`) is\n    `model = raw.get(\"model\") or fallback_default or _DEFAULT_MODEL_BY_CLASS[name]`, where\n    `fallback_default` is the deprecated bare `REBAR_LLM_MODEL`. With that env var set, an\n    UNCONFIGURED class resolves to it for ALL THREE classes — so \"empty table ⇒ sonnet\" is only\n    true when `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is unset. (Measured here: it IS unset on this host, so this is not\n    the live mechanism — but the inference was unsound regardless.)\n 2. **MEASURED, and it reframes the whole live probe: this repo configures NO `model_classes`\n    table at all** (`_read_llm_file_table(None)` returns `{}`), so the built-in defaults apply and\n    `frontier` → `anthropic:claude-opus-4-8` — which IS `cfg.model` (`DEFAULT_MODEL` is\n    `claude-opus-4-8`). Therefore `base`, `round_a` and `round_b` (all declaring `frontier`) are\n    EXPECTED to run on opus, honoured or not. Those calls carried no information. The ONLY\n    discriminating steps in the whole gate are `verify` and `coach`, which declare `standard`\n    (→ sonnet). \"Nine of nine on opus\" was six-plus uninformative rows plus two real ones.\n\n**THE OFFLINE TEST I WROTE IS GREEN, AND THAT IS ALSO A FINDING, NOT A FAILURE.**\n`tests/unit/workflow/test_code_review_step_model_attribution.py` runs the REAL gate doc through the\nreal interpreter with the production `RunnerAgentStep` and a fake runner beneath it (the b690\ncapture point: `req.config.model` at `runner.run(req)`), under a class table whose three values\ndiffer from `cfg.model`. All seven assertions pass, and a mutation check\n(`resolve_model(cfg, step=None, workflow=None)`) turns five of them red naming all five steps — so\nthe test is sound and capable, and it PROVES the interpreter → `RunnerAgentStep` → `resolve_model`\n→ `CodeReviewBatchRunner` chain is correct. The capture point named in this ticket's earlier\ncomment is one layer too high to see the actual defect: it observes the value the runner is\nhanded, and the bug is that the runner ignores what it is handed. Keeping that test (it pins the\nupper half) and adding one BENEATH the model resolution.\n\n**SCOPE WIDENS, AND THIS NEEDS A DECISION.** The defect is in the shared runner, not in\ncode-review, so it affects EVERY workflow that declares a per-step model — plan-review and the\ncompletion gate too. That also retires the \"asymmetry lead\": plan-review only LOOKED like it\nhonoured classes because with no class table `frontier` == `cfg.model` == opus, so its 41/1\nopus/sonnet split is explained without `gate_config` having anything to do with it. Next: design\nthe fix at `runner.py`, which is OUTSIDE the three files step 5 anticipated\n(gate_dispatch/runs/batch_runner) — checking the plan's Scope and file_impact before editing, since\na fix landing in an unpinned file is exactly the kind of plan/implementation divergence the review\ngate blocks on."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785622749633866001,"uuid":"fd4f9ce0-62f1-4b4a-9eb6-883ac5731fb8"}